module std.machine_constraints

import std.constructors { Phantom }

// A TYPE ARGUMENT IS NOT A QUANTITY AND NOT A VALUE. What stands in MachineWidth's parameter
// position is WHICH width this type is -- a type-level index. std.measure BitWidth
// (Measure<Information, One, Nat>) is the measured amount of bits and is what REIFYING a width
// PRODUCES, not what the argument denotes; putting it here was this declaration's first draft and a
// category error. A value-carrying coproduct was its second: see the enumeration below.
//
// WHAT THIS POSITION ADMITS: a Nat literal, or the named type PointerWidth. The wall checks the
// NAMED half against this declared kind; literal admissibility is a language-level rule rather than
// a per-kind fact, which is a DECLARED FRONTIER stated at machine_width_literal_roster_frontier
// below rather than a silent gap.
//
// FIVE CARRIERS WERE TRIED FOR A PER-KIND LITERAL ROSTER AND EVERY ONE FAILED ON THE SAME AXIS.
// Recorded so the next reader does not re-walk it:
//   payload (StaticWidth { bits: Nat })  -- value-level in a type-level position: E0573 at the 4
//     live MachineWidth<PointerWidth> sites, a generated panic! arm on the field accessor, and a
//     record nothing ever constructs because the corpus writes a bare 32.
//   arm named for the type      -- a coproduct arm PointerWidth beside `type PointerWidth` puts two
//     claimants on one name in one module; which wins decides the emission, so it is
//     resolution-order dependent.
//   alias                       -- carries named inhabitants cleanly but cannot carry Nat without
//     minting arms, which is the collision above.
//   data row                    -- no resolver reads data initializers: ModuleItemDataValue appears
//     nowhere in v1.compiler.infer_resolve, infer_env or infer, and the one precedent that consumes
//     a symbol-index hit reads decl.inferred, the declared TYPE, not the initializer.
//   bare unit variants          -- EMITS AND COMPILES, verified cross-module with markers minted and
//     a correctly qualified use line, and STILL cannot express per-kind literal admissibility: a
//     bare 32 is a literal, not a name, so admitting it needs a compiler-held convention mapping
//     literals to an arm spelled StaticWidth. That is the stringly proxy this wall exists to remove.
// The last entry is the one worth reading: it is the option that looks viable and is not.

// THE KIND, AND ITS ARMS ARE THE ROSTER. The check asks whether the argument is one of these
// DECLARED arms, so a kind naming PointerWidth cannot be satisfied by an unrelated declaration that
// happens to have the right shape -- which is the difference between this and the variant-shape
// proxy it replaced.
// IT IS A COPRODUCT RATHER THAN AN ALIAS FOR A MEASURED REASON. `type WidthResolution = PointerWidth`
// is a SINGLE-ARM form, which this language parses as a TYPE ALIAS; the declaration then reaches the
// kind check as a bare nominal node carrying neither a resolved target nor children, so the check
// could read nothing and refused its own declared inhabitant. The refusal printed it:
//   'PointerWidth [decl=WidthResolution inferred=absent resolved_target= children=0]'
// Two arms make it a real coproduct whose children ARE readable. Unit variants in type-argument
// position are minted as zero-sized markers and compile, verified cross-module with a correctly
// generated qualified use line, so PointerWidth keeps working at the 4 live MachineWidth sites.
// StaticWidthIndex names the literal arm so the roster is complete rather than implicit; a Nat
// literal is admitted by the language-level rule described above, not by matching this arm.
type WidthResolution
  = StaticWidthIndex
  | PointerWidth

type MachineWidth<bits: WidthResolution>

type Compose<Algebra, MachineConstraint> = Phantom
